Package: openmux-net-tools
Version: 1.0.0
Architecture: all
Maintainer: OpenMux <support@openmux.io>
Homepage: https://github.com/OpenMux
Section: net
Priority: optional
Depends: netplan.io, iproute2, systemd
Description: Operator tool for the wired address on end0
 set-ip shows what netplan and the running system currently say about end0, asks
 for dhcp or static, and for static asks for the address with its prefix length,
 the gateway and the DNS servers. It writes one file of its own,
 /etc/netplan/90-openmux-end0.yaml, and edits no other: netplan merges every file
 in that directory per key with the lexically later file winning, so the
 addressing the image ships in 01-openmux.yaml is overridden without touching it,
 and removing this one file is the way back.
 .
 The file is rewritten whole rather than edited, because netplan merges an
 addresses/routes/nameservers list by adding to whatever an earlier file set, and
 a later file cannot take one away - so the files it has to coexist with are
 reported before anything is written, and anything the tool does not ask about
 (extra routes, search domains) belongs in a file sorted after its own.
 .
 Before applying it runs the real generator against the merge it is about to
 create, in a temporary root, and prints the unit the daemon will be given.
 Applying restarts the daemon that owns end0, which over SSH is the session the
 command is typed in, so the rollback is a systemd timer rather than something in
 the tool's own process: it restores the previous file and re-applies it unless the
 operator confirms, and it still fires when the session that armed it is gone.
 .
 netplan.io provides the parser the file is validated with and the renderer the
 answer is read from; iproute2 provides the live address, route and reachability
 report; systemd provides networkctl, which says who owns the port, and
 systemd-run, which arms the rollback. resolvectl is used to show the DNS servers
 when systemd-resolved is present, and its absence is not fatal.
